  "query_preview": "(Today through Monday)...\nIssued at 233 AM EDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nSurface analysis early this morning shows strong high pressure \ncentered over Ontario. This large high was extending its influence \nsouth, across Indiana and through the Ohio Valley to the deep south. \nAloft, water vapor showed a ridge axis extending north across \nIndiana and the Great Lakes, while an upper trough was found over OK \nand Central TX. Water Vapor showed a plume of moisture streaming \nnorth across the southern plains to the middle Mississippi Valley \nstreaming northward. Dew points across Indiana ranged from the \nmiddle 40s to around 50.\n\nToday through Wednesday...\n\nThe strong surface high to the north is expected to slide southeast \ntoday and Wednesday reaching east of New England by Wednesday, all \nwhile continuing to maintain control over Indiana/s weather. Warmer \nsoutherly return flow is expected today and again on Wednesday. \nMeanwhile aloft, the ridge axis aloft will also quickly depart east \nallowing the plains trough to push across Indiana tonight. This will \nbe followed by another weak ridge for Wednesday. Forecast soundings \nthrough Wednesday show a continued dry column, with little overall \nchange in the airmass.\n\nGiven GOES19 trends, mostly sunny skies will be expected this \nmorning, but passing high and mid clouds as seen over the mid \nMississippi valley will be expected to pass through the afternoon as \nthe upper flow progresses. This will bring some partly cloudy skies \nthis afternoon.\n\nMore sunshine will be expected on Wednesday as the second ridge axis \narrives providing subsidence. \n\nThursday...\n\nA dry and even warmer day is expected on Thursday. A cold front \npushing across the Great Lakes will provide SW flow across Indiana \nthrough the day on Thursday as models suggest a thermal ridge to be \nin place across the state. Best forcing and moisture with this front \nwill remain well to the northeast, across the eastern Great Lakes. \nThus we will expect partly cloudy skies with warm SW winds. This \nshould allow highs to reach the upper 70s.\n\nFriday...\n\nDry weather will be expected on Friday as strong ridging aloft will \nremain over Indiana, Illinois and Iowa. Resultant surface high \npressure is expected over the Great Lakes drifting through the \nsoutheast. Forecast soundings through the day show mainly a dry \ncolumn. Thus dry weather is expected with continued above normal \ntemperatures.\n\nSaturday through Monday...\n\nA wetter period is expected on Saturday through Monday. The upper \nflow is expected to become more southwesterly across the CONUS as a \ndeep upper low begins to dig southeast into the northwestern states. \nThis will bring a progressive flow across the American southwest, \nacross the plains and to the Ohio valley. Embedded forcing dynamics \namid the flow and a short wave will push toward and across Central \nIndiana on Saturday through Sunday Night. Within the lower levels \nstrong high pressure will be found across the middle Atlantic states \nwhile low pressure over the southern plains pushes towards Indiana \non Saturday and across the state on Sunday. This should set up a \nsteady stream of moisture into the Ohio Valley. Another warm front \nlooks to push toward Indiana on Monday. Forecast soundings through \nthis period show periods of deep saturation with pwats around 1.25 \ninches. These are some of the higher values than we have seen in \nrecent weeks. Thus mid to high pops will be used, with the best \nchances on Saturday night into Sunday. Due to the cloud cover and \nprecipitation during the period, expect lows in the 50s, but highs \nmainly reaching only the mid to upper 60s. Normal high temperatures \nfor this time of year is in the upper 50s.",
